WEBVTT

00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:06.365
Reading a SQL query someone else wrote is harder than writing one yourself.

00:00:06.365 --> 00:00:10.731
The query is a single statement, but it runs in a specific order, and the order is not the order you read in.

00:00:10.731 --> 00:00:15.096
To understand a complex query, you have to mentally execute it the way the database does.

00:00:15.096 --> 00:00:19.462
There is a method for that.

00:00:19.462 --> 00:00:23.827
Two examples make it concrete.

00:00:23.827 --> 00:00:31.830
Example one: for each user, find genres they have two or more songs in.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:39.833
The query joins Listens to Songs to attach genre, drops songs with NULL genre, groups by user and genre, then keeps only groups with COUNT of star greater than or equal to two.

00:00:39.833 --> 00:00:47.836
Six clauses, but they execute in a specific order, and that order is the key to reading the query.

00:00:47.836 --> 00:00:52.033
Watch the table shrink as you walk.

00:00:52.033 --> 00:00:56.230
FROM Listens starts at nine rows.

00:00:56.230 --> 00:01:00.427
JOIN Songs adds genre to each row, still nine.

00:01:00.427 --> 00:01:04.624
WHERE genre IS NOT NULL drops nothing here, since every listen happens to point to a non-null-genre song.

00:01:04.624 --> 00:01:08.821
GROUP BY user id and genre collapses to six rows, one per user-genre pair.

00:01:08.821 --> 00:01:13.018
HAVING COUNT of star greater than or equal to two drops three of those, leaving three rows.

00:01:13.018 --> 00:01:17.215
SELECT picks the columns, ORDER BY sorts them.

00:01:17.215 --> 00:01:21.412
The query executed in your head.

00:01:21.412 --> 00:01:27.635
Example two: find the top-half users by average rating.

00:01:27.635 --> 00:01:33.858
The query has a common table expression for per-user metrics, a window function for percentile rank, and a final top-half filter.

00:01:33.858 --> 00:01:40.081
More moving parts than example one, but the same mental discipline applies.

00:01:40.081 --> 00:01:45.422
Walk it clause by clause.

00:01:45.422 --> 00:01:50.762
The first CTE builds per-user metrics: one row per user with their listen count and average rating.

00:01:50.762 --> 00:01:56.103
The second CTE adds a percentile rank using a window function over avg rating descending, so the highest rating gets rank zero.

00:01:56.103 --> 00:02:01.444
The outer query filters to the top half: rating rank less than zero point five.

00:02:01.444 --> 00:02:06.784
On our toy data with three users, that surfaces Minnie at four point four.

00:02:06.784 --> 00:02:12.125
Three nested layers, each one a small transformation, ending at the answer.

00:02:12.125 --> 00:02:18.580
Reading a query is mental execution.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:25.036
FROM, WHERE, GROUP BY, HAVING, SELECT, ORDER BY, LIMIT, in that order, with the table looking different after each one.

00:02:25.036 --> 00:02:31.491
Once you can do this for someone else's query, you can also debug your own.

00:02:31.491 --> 00:02:37.946
The discipline applies whether the query is fifteen lines or fifty: walk it clause by clause and watch the data change.
